<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abhiraja</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abhiraja is the legendary founder of the kingdom of Tagaung in Burmese chronicles. The chronicles say he was a prince of the Sakya clan, the Buddha's kin, who migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and founded Tagaung on the upper Irrawaddy, traditionally in the 9th century BCE, and that his descendants later founded Sri Ksetra. The story links Burmese kingship to the Buddha's lineage and is not historical.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hmannan-yazawin">Hmannan Yazawin</a>, Abhiraja was a prince of the ancient kingdom of Kosala in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and lord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapilavastu">Kapilavastu</a> region, the birthplace of the historical Buddha three centuries later. He descended from Maha Sammata, the first Buddhist king. When the king of Panchala was rudely refused the hand of the Kosala king's daughter, he conquered Kosala, and its ruling clan scattered in three directions. Abhiraja led his followers over a long mountainous route to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and founded a kingdom at Tagaung in 850 BCE.</p><p>The chronicle does not claim that the land was empty, only that he was its first king. Of his two sons, the elder, Kanyaza Gyi, went south and founded a kingdom in Arakan in 825 BCE, while the younger, Kanyaza Nge, succeeded him at the head of a dynasty of 31 kings. Scions of Tagaung founded Sri Ksetra in 483 BCE, and the chronicle traces Pagan to Thamoddarit, nephew of its last king, around 107 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The story first appeared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hmannan-yazawin">Hmannan Yazawin</a>, the Glass Palace Chronicle, completed in 1832. Earlier chronicles, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maha-yazawin">Maha Yazawin</a> of 1724 on which it largely rests, do not mention Abhiraja and trace the monarchy to Pyusawhti, son of a solar spirit and a dragon princess. Historians trace the story to the 1770s and the early Konbaung kings' promotion of a more orthodox <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>, a drive that gained ground under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodawpaya">Bodawpaya</a>. In 1832, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagyidaw">Bagyidaw</a>, the Pyusawhti story was officially superseded. Dali, Lan Na, Nan and the Shan chronicles likewise linked their rulers to the Buddha or to Asoka.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the British colonial period the story damaged the credibility of the chronicles' early history. European scholars dismissed it as copies of Indian legends and doubted that any civilisation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> was much older than 500 CE; Phayre in 1883 placed Abhiraja and the Tagaung line in a list of kings of legend, and Harvey in 1925 left him out altogether. The historian Than Tun blamed the chroniclers for giving colonial historians grounds to denigrate Burmese history. Modern research shows that many places named in the royal records have been inhabited for at least 3500 years, and that Tagaung, Sri Ksetra and Pagan all existed, though not in the chronicles' linear order.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abhiyaza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Burmese chronicles, compiled many centuries later, say that <b>Abhiraja</b>, a prince of the Sakya clan of the Buddha, migrated from India and founded Tagaung on the east bank of the Irrawaddy, traditionally dated to the 9th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/tagaung-kingdom">Tagaung Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kapilvastu Municipality, in what is now <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>. Died in Tagaung, Mandalay, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.54,83.05" aria-label="Kapilvastu Municipality on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Abhiraja"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4475 5194h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4475 5194h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27.54,83.05">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
